WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.700
Every second,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.477
this antenna hands the radio one hundred ninety-two thousand numbers.

00:00:07.777 --> 00:00:10.550
Somewhere inside them is a song.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.474
How do we get it out?

00:00:13.024 --> 00:00:15.239
A song is a slow wiggle.

00:00:15.539 --> 00:00:19.735
To travel far, it rides a fast wave called the carrier,

00:00:19.435 --> 00:00:22.144
and pushes its height up and down.

00:00:22.444 --> 00:00:23.974
That's AM.

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:26.276
But the air is crowded.

00:00:26.576 --> 00:00:29.735
Four stations and the static arrive together,

00:00:29.435 --> 00:00:32.785
and the antenna adds them into one tangled line.

00:00:33.335 --> 00:00:38.233
Spread that line out by frequency, and each station becomes a peak.

00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:40.962
Multiply by a local oscillator,

00:00:40.662 --> 00:00:44.671
and the peak you want slides to twenty-four kilohertz,

00:00:44.371 --> 00:00:47.006
where one sharp filter is waiting.

00:00:47.556 --> 00:00:49.377
Now follow the tops.

00:00:49.677 --> 00:00:53.432
A diode keeps each peak, a capacitor holds it,

00:00:53.132 --> 00:00:56.211
and the outline it draws is the song.

00:00:56.511 --> 00:00:59.308
The sound was the change all along.

00:00:59.858 --> 00:01:04.894
Drive away, and the wave shrinks, so static hides in its height.

00:01:05.194 --> 00:01:08.722
FM carries the song in the spacing instead.

00:01:09.022 --> 00:01:14.276
A limiter flattens every height, and when two stations share a channel,

00:01:13.976 --> 00:01:16.927
the radio locks onto the stronger one.

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:19.425
Pick one wave.

00:01:19.725 --> 00:01:21.825
Move it to your filter.

00:01:22.125 --> 00:01:24.410
Measure how it changes.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.843
That's a radio, built from numbers.
